  "id": "V035",
  "description": "An explicit 'no preference' pair vote (winner_id null) and a missing pair vote both contribute to neither side of the head-to-head",
  "spec_clause": "Phase 2 / Pairwise comparison: 'Allow skip / no preference - forcing a choice between two films someone hasn't seen produces noise, not information.' + Data model PairVote: 'winner_id | null -- null = explicit no preference'",
  "rationale": "5 attendees. m1: 3 rows, all yes -> coverage 3/5 = 0.60 (at floor), yes 3, approval 3/3 = 1.00. m2: 5 rows, 4 yes -> coverage 1.00, yes 4, approval 0.80. Ranked by yes-votes: m2 (4), m1 (3); both clear the floor, no vetoes, both survive. The single pair is stored with movie_a = m1, movie_b = m2: u1 -> m1, u2 -> m1, u3 -> m2, u4 -> explicit null (no preference), u5 -> no PairVote row at all. Tally: m1 preferred 2, m2 preferred 1, no_preference 1, not_voted 1. Only strict preferences count, so 2 > 1 -> m1 beats m2 and is the Condorcet winner. Crediting the null and/or the missing row to movie_b would give m2 3 to 2 and flip the result; crediting them to both sides would also be wrong (the totals must sum to 3 strict preferences, not 5).",
